ner is bound to the health command's hardcoded host:port (or how the extracted URL is rewritten to the test listener's actual address) so the executed command provably reaches the controlled socket.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.0044,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["docker-compose.yml:291 and :529 and Dockerfile.mcp:106 pin the command to a literal 127.0.0.1:8091/:8000","Approach: 'Execute the configured health command against controlled good and bad listeners so the oracle observes a real socket and response'","AC3: 'The real configured health command succeeds against a valid listener...'"],"finding":"The plan proposes executing the configured health command against a test-controlled listener, but the configured command hardcodes host:port (127.0.0.1:8091 for mcp, 127.0.0.1:8000 for review-bot); the plan does not specify how the controlled good/bad listener binds that exact fixed port (or how the extracted URL is reconciled with a test-chosen port), so the 'real command hits the controlled socket' mechanism is under-specified and may not actually exercise the listener.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f909aa0f5fbdad2d0","impact":0.67,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach / Testing / AC3","norm_id":"n0b6679dc0c56ee70","priority":0.5956,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["The health command's fixed port is already in use, forbidden, or occupied by another test's listener, so binding it is flaky or impossible","The test binds a random free port but the extracted command still targets the hardcoded port, so it never reaches the controlled listener"],"severity":"major","suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.8889,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"yes","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"underspecified_oracle","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"medium","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"underspecified_certification","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"medium","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"medium"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"Note that the shipped Dockerfiles are single-stage and state that the parser must correctly select the final stage for both the real single-stage files and the added multi-stage broken fixture, so AC1's guarantee covers the shipped artifacts and not only the fixture.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.005,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Dockerfile.mcp:16 and Dockerfile.reviewbot:24 each contain a single unnamed `FROM python:3.12-slim` with no later `FROM` or `AS`","AC1: 'A broken shipped final stage fails even when an earlier stage retains all expected tokens.'"],"finding":"The shipped Dockerfile.mcp and Dockerfile.reviewbot are single-stage (one `FROM`, no `AS`/multi-stage). The plan's 'parse the shipped final Docker stage' framing and AC1 ('a broken shipped final stage fails even when an earlier stage retains all expected tokens') describe a multi-stage scenario that the real files do not exhibit; the guarantee therefore only bites on the added synthetic fixture, and the plan does not state that the parser must still correctly select the sole/final stage of the actual single-stage files.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f266acc607e0ec247","impact":0.85,"is_primary":null,"location"